      "title": "The Silent Colonizers",
      "passage": "When a species of freshwater mussel is carried beyond its native range and released into a foreign lake, ecologists rarely notice its arrival. The animals are small, sedentary, and easily mistaken for harmless debris on the lakebed. Yet within a few years such newcomers can transform an entire ecosystem. Because they filter enormous volumes of water in search of microscopic algae, dense colonies gradually strip the water of the very particles on which native fish larvae depend. The consequences are not immediately visible, but the removal of this food source §23§ the populations of species that had flourished in the lake for millennia.\n\nWhat makes these invaders so formidable is a combination of traits that native mussels seldom possess together. They mature early, reproduce in vast numbers, and tolerate a wide range of temperatures and pollutants. In an unfamiliar habitat that lacks the predators and parasites which once kept them in check, these advantages compound. Freed from the pressures of their homeland, the colonizers can §24§ their competitors, monopolizing space on hard surfaces until little room remains for anything else.\n\nManagers have tried poisons, physical barriers, and even the deliberate introduction of predatory fish, but each remedy carries its own hazards, and none has proved decisive once a lake is thoroughly colonized. Prevention, most biologists now argue, is the only reliable defense. Boats and equipment must be inspected and cleaned before they move between waterways, since a single overlooked larva can found a new population. The lesson drawn from decades of failed eradication campaigns is therefore sobering: §25§.",
