      "title": "The Economy of the Fresco",
      "passage": "In the prosperous city-states of an imagined early-modern republic, the grandest wall paintings were seldom the private whim of the artists who executed them. A large fresco was expensive, immovable, and highly visible, and it was almost always commissioned by a guild, a religious confraternity, or a wealthy merchant family. For the patron, the work was less an object of contemplation than a public statement of standing. The choice of subject, the costly pigments, and the sheer scale of the commission all §26§ the resources and ambitions of those who paid for it.\n\nThis dependence on patronage shaped the very style of the paintings. Because a fresco had to be completed while the plaster was still wet, the artist could not endlessly revise; every figure had to be planned in advance and executed with speed and confidence. Painters who mastered this demanding technique were in high demand, and their workshops trained apprentices in methods that could be passed down. §27§, the constraints of the medium fostered a distinctive discipline that came to define the period's finest work.\n\nWhen the republic's economy faltered in later generations, the flow of commissions dwindled, and the tradition entered a long decline. Historians once attributed this decline chiefly to a supposed exhaustion of artistic imagination. More recent scholarship, however, has §28§ that explanation, pointing instead to the collapse of the merchant fortunes that had sustained the workshops. The frescoes, in this view, faded not because painters ran out of ideas, but because the patrons who commissioned them had run out of money.",
