    "article": "In many densely populated cities, empty lots, disused railway strips, and even rooftops are quietly being converted into small community gardens and pocket parks. Local authorities have embraced this trend enthusiastically, presenting it as an inexpensive means of addressing several urban problems at once. Advocates argue that patches of greenery cool overheated neighborhoods, absorb rainwater that would otherwise overwhelm drains, filter polluted air, and offer stressed residents a rare opportunity to encounter plants and wildlife without leaving the city. For municipalities operating under tight budgets, encouraging citizens and developers to create such spaces appears to be an appealingly cheap and popular policy.\n\nYet a growing number of researchers have begun to question whether the benefits are shared as widely as officials suggest. Studies indicate that new green spaces tend to cluster in wealthier districts, while poorer areas remain comparatively barren. More troubling still, when neglected neighborhoods are finally given attractive parks, property values and rents in the surrounding streets frequently rise. Longtime residents on modest incomes may then find themselves priced out and forced to move elsewhere, a phenomenon that critics have labeled green gentrification. In such cases, the very people the projects were meant to help end up excluded from them.\n\nConscious of these criticisms, some city governments have started to redesign their approach. A number now oblige property developers to finance publicly accessible plots as a condition of approval, and insist that neighborhood residents help decide how the land is used. Supporters believe that such measures can distribute the advantages of urban greenery more evenly and prevent displacement. Others remain skeptical, cautioning that unless communities retain genuine long-term control over the spaces and adequate funds are set aside for their upkeep, well-intentioned gardens may still fail to serve those who need them most.",
