    "modelEssay": "Governments around the world are increasingly considering whether cities should provide free public transportation to all residents. I firmly agree that making public transport free would benefit society as a whole, for reasons relating to the environment, the economy, and social equity.\n\nFirstly, abolishing fares would substantially reduce environmental damage. When commuting costs nothing, many drivers abandon their private vehicles, thereby curbing the emissions that aggravate urban air pollution. For instance, a metropolis plagued by chronic congestion could witness a marked decline in harmful pollutants once buses and trains become a compelling alternative to cars.\n\nFurthermore, free transit would stimulate economic activity. Residents who save on daily fares tend to spend that money elsewhere, invigorating local businesses. Moreover, employers benefit when workers can commute affordably from distant, cheaper neighbourhoods, widening the pool of available labour and alleviating downtown overcrowding.\n\nFinally, eliminating fares promotes social equity. Transportation costs disproportionately burden low-income citizens, effectively restricting their access to employment, education, and healthcare. By rendering mobility universally accessible, a city ensures that opportunity is not reserved solely for the affluent, fostering a more inclusive community.\n\nIn conclusion, although funding such a scheme poses genuine fiscal challenges, the environmental, economic, and social advantages render free public transportation a worthwhile investment. For these reasons, I am convinced that cities should provide it to every resident without charge.",
